// Recipe content (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.6) — data, never hardcoded in sim_core.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader must reject or migrate a mismatched version rather than
// silently misreading a changed shape.
//
// Milestone 4 (First full chain) turned this into a real multi-step
// production line. Since the BBQ epic (2026-09-18) it is a **production
// GRAPH**: the ketchup entries still run head to tail in file order, and the
// BBQ entries after them hang off the middle of that line rather than
// continuing it.
//
// **WHAT THIS FILE'S ORDER MEANS.** `World` builds nothing from it: the
// player places every belt tile, pipe tile and machine, and flow is derived
// from what ends up adjacent to what. So the order is not *geometry* — but
// it is still content that three things read.
//
// (1) `validate_chain` checks that every part of this file is content a
// player can **reach**: each connected component of the recipe graph has to
// hold at least one recipe that can be started — its machine is for sale in
// `data/shop.ron` and everything it consumes is obtainable, where
// "obtainable" grows outwards from what `data/ingredients.ron` prices. So a
// typo'd input id is still caught at load rather than showing up as a
// machine that silently never starts. **It does not demand that the graph be
// one component** (issue #42): it once required consecutive entries to
// chain, then that every recipe be reachable from the first, and the file
// outgrew both. A fork is legal, and so is a whole second line that shares
// nothing — which is what mayonnaise will be (GAMEPLAY.md §2) and what the
// mustard tier below nearly is.
//
// (2) An item that some recipe consumes and no recipe produces is by
// definition a *raw ingredient*, which is what a hopper holds and what
// `data/ingredients.ron` has to price. That rule is what makes finished
// `ketchup` NOT a raw even though the BBQ mixer consumes it.
//
// (3) `sim::reference_chain` — the test fixture that rebuilds the shipped
// layout out of ordinary purchase and placement commands — lays out the
// **linear prefix** of this file, i.e. the ketchup line, and stops at the
// first entry that does not continue it. The BBQ branch has its own fixture
// (`sim::bbq_branch`), because a branch is not something a one-row,
// west-to-east layout rule can express. Adding a processing step to the
// ketchup line is therefore still a pure data-file edit; adding a new
// *branch* needs a fixture to go with it.
//
// **A MACHINE KIND MAY NAME SEVERAL RECIPES** (BBQ epic; this used to say
// "exactly one"). A placed machine re-picks, on each idle tick, whichever of
// its kind's recipes its current inputs satisfy, with THIS FILE'S ORDER as
// the deterministic tiebreak. There is still no recipe-selection mechanic
// and deliberately no player-facing picker: what a machine makes is decided
// by what you plumb into it.
//
// **WHY THE ORDER BELOW IS SAFE.** The tiebreak only bites when one
// machine's current inputs satisfy two of its kind's recipes at once, and on
// the shipped content that cannot happen: any pair of recipes sharing a
// machine kind takes **completely disjoint input ids**, fluids included.
//
//   crusher      tomato  /  mustard_seed + vinegar
//   mixer        tomato_paste + sugar + vinegar
//                  /  spice_mix + molasses + ketchup
//                  /  turmeric + mustard_mash
//   cooking_vat  ketchup_mix  /  bbq_mix + liquid_smoke  /  mustard_mix
//   bottler      ketchup  /  bbq  /  mustard
//   packer       bottled_ketchup  /  bottled_bbq  /  bottled_mustard
//
// A machine only ever holds what the player plumbed into it, so a mixer
// plumbed for one of those three rows is missing everything each of the
// other two demands: fed paste and sugar it cannot satisfy `mustard_mix`
// (no turmeric, no mash), and fed turmeric and mash it cannot satisfy
// `ketchup_mix`. File order therefore still decides nothing, and reordering
// this file could not change what any machine makes. `recipe.rs`'s
// `recipes_on_a_kind_take_disjoint_inputs` pins exactly that, and the day
// two recipes on one kind DO overlap, a player gets a machine whose output
// depends on a file order they cannot see — the point at which this design
// needs revisiting rather than a quiet tiebreak.
//
// **MUSTARD IS THE NEAREST MISS SO FAR**, and it is worth saying where it
// nearly went. `vinegar` is the first raw two sauces buy (point 6 of
// `data/ingredients.ron`'s note), and mustard's first draft drank it at the
// MIXER, beside turmeric — which would have put the same input id on two
// recipes of one kind and broken the rule above. It drinks at the CRUSHER
// instead, which drank nothing, and the mustard mixer takes the derived
// `mustard_mash` down a pipe the way the BBQ mixer takes derived `ketchup`.
// Disjointness alone would have allowed other ways out — a second acid of
// mustard's own, say — and the rule in the next paragraph is what ruled
// those out and left this one.
//
// **AT MOST ONE RAW FLUID PER MACHINE KIND, DRUNK BY AT MOST ONE OF ITS
// RECIPES.** This one is not a style preference, it is what
// `World::resolve_network_fluids` can actually work out, and breaking it
// breaks a line that already ships. That pass names a pipe run by narrowing
// on FLUIDS ALONE — the solid side of a recipe is invisible to it — and the
// only fact that pins a run from outside is "a run a tank feeds holds a raw
// fluid". So:
//
//   * two recipes on one kind drinking the SAME raw fluid leaves that kind's
//     OUTLET run ambiguous (both recipes stay feasible, so nothing narrows);
//   * one kind whose recipes drink two DIFFERENT raw fluids leaves the TANK
//     run feeding it ambiguous, and an ambiguous tank run cannot even be
//     filled (`World::tank_fluid` has nothing to name it with).
//
// Both were measured while writing this tier, on the shipped ketchup line,
// in a new game where mustard is still gated — a gate hides content from the
// player, not from the resolver. Hence mustard's vinegar goes in at the
// crusher, which drinks nothing else, and the mixer takes the derived
// `mustard_mash` the way `bbq_mix` takes derived `ketchup`. The fence for
// sauce four is `recipe.rs`'s `at_most_one_raw_fluid_enters_any_machine_kind`;
// the principled fix — teaching the resolver about the solid side — is
// issue #184, not something to work around in content.
//
// FLUIDS, BOTH WAYS (schema_version 3). Milestone 6 added the optional
// `fluid_inputs` field — (fluid id, whole units) drawn per craft from the
// pipe network the machine touches (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.4). The chain-rework
// epic adds its mirror, `fluid_outputs`: whole units *deposited* into a
// network at the end of a craft. Both default to empty, so a recipe that
// names neither is an ordinary belt-in/belt-out stage.
//
// **A recipe may draw SEVERAL fluids** (BBQ epic; the old one-fluid-input
// cap is gone). Each entry in `fluid_inputs` is matched at tick time to a
// touching pipe run carrying that fluid id. Fluid *outputs* are still capped
// at one, and it still leaves by the outlet face. The BBQ cook is the recipe
// that needed this: it draws piped mix and tanked liquid smoke at once.
//
// Together they are what lets the middle of this chain be **piped rather
// than belted**, which is what a real sauce line looks like: crushed paste
// and sugar arrive on a conveyor, and from the moment they are blended into
// a liquid mix until the moment that liquid is put in a bottle, the product
// moves through pipe, not on a belt. So the mixer's output and the cooking
// vat's output are fluids, the bottler is where the product becomes a solid
// again, and the two stages in between have no belt attached to them at
// all.
//
// A machine deposits into at most one network, and draws from one network
// per fluid it needs (`validate_fluid_scope`), and which is which is
// geometric: **a fluid output leaves by the face the machine points at,
// exactly as a solid one does; a fluid input may come from any other pipe
// touching the footprint.** See `sim_core/src/sim/world.rs`'s module doc.
//
// The ketchup entries below are a simplified stand-in for GAMEPLAY.md §2's
// tier 1/2 tomato-sauce/ketchup progression, and the BBQ entries its tier 3.
// Neither is a final recipe; this file keeps every stage to a shape the
// current topology can express (no belt-side convergence beyond what a
// hopper standing beside a feed belt already provides).
//
// PROCESS TIMES follow the work each machine does, so the line has a
// visible bottleneck rather than a row of interchangeable stages. Of the
// ketchup line's three front-end processing stages: crushing is mechanical
// and the quickest of them (80 ticks — under three seconds at
// ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9's 30 ticks/sec), blending is a little slower still
// (90), and the cook-down is the longest step on THAT line (240, three
// times the crush) — which is precisely why the cooking vat is also the
// biggest machine on it (`data/machines.ron`).
//
// **The ORDER of those three is the claim, and it is load-bearing**: this
// comment, `data/machines.ron`'s cooking-vat note and CLAUDE.md's decisions
// log all say in words that crushing is the quickest of them and the cook
// the slowest, so a number that inverts the order makes those sentences
// false. Change the numbers and the words together or not at all. The crush
// was 60 until the 2026-09-09 tuning pass, which raised it because at 60 it
// read as instant beside its neighbours — a stage the player never sees
// working is a stage that does not look like it is doing anything. 80 keeps
// it comfortably under the mixer's 90, so the crusher still out-produces the
// stage it feeds and the ketchup line's bottleneck stays where it was
// designed to be (the vat).
//
// The **BBQ cook at 600 is now the longest process in the game**, two and a
// half times the ketchup cook, and it is deliberately not on the ketchup
// line: it sits on the branch, so it slows BBQ production without touching
// the shipped line's 240-tick bottleneck at all. A player who never builds
// a BBQ branch sees exactly the timings they saw before. See that recipe's
// own note for why 600 specifically.
//
// **The mustard cook sits between the two at 360**, and it is the only one
// of the three whose number is argued from a *ratio to another line* rather
// than from the work in the pot. A mustard line is a whole second line from
// raw, and its own bottleneck is its vat, so a factory running one finished
// ketchup line and one finished mustard line settles at 240:360 — three
// cases of ketchup to two of mustard, which is the mix `deli_chain` asks
// for in `data/contracts.ron`. That makes it load-bearing exactly the way
// 600 is; see the `mustard` entry below for the whole argument.
//
// WHAT WAS REMOVED. This file used to carry a sixth stage, `aged_ketchup`
// on an `aging_rack`, added by ARCHITECTURE.md §9 milestone 7 to prove the
// generic `TimedProcess` at a 2400-tick timescale. Ageing is not a mechanic
// this game wants, so the stage is gone — deleted rather than disabled.
// `TimedProcess` itself is untouched and still runs every craft cycle here.
//
// WHAT THE COOKBOOK READS. `tagline`, `blurb`, `icon` and `cookbook_icon`
// are all optional, and they exist so that THE COOKBOOK — the in-game
// encyclopedia on B — is written here rather than in GDScript. A sauce added
// to this file gets a page with no Rust edit and no Godot edit: the tree, the
// ingredient list, the machine process, the timings and the cost per box are
// all derived from the fields above it, and these are the only parts a
// machine cannot work out for itself.
//
// Two rules about them, both learned the hard way elsewhere in this repo.
//
// (1) A BLURB NEVER QUOTES A NUMBER THIS FILE ALREADY OWNS. Writing "four
// tomatoes" or "eight seconds" into prose makes a second copy of `inputs`
// and `time_ticks` that no test compares, so the next balance pass leaves
// the page lying. The page prints the real numbers beside the prose; the
// prose says what they mean.
//
// (2) `icon` NAMES A VOXEL ASSET, and it is how `main.gd`'s `ITEM_MODELS`
// table stops having to grow. That table is a hand-written map from content
// id to asset name in a file that ships green when it goes stale — the
// failure UI_UX.md §2b point 12 records. The wire is consulted first now,
// so content names its own picture and the table is only the fallback for
// ids written before this field existed.
//
// (3) `cookbook_icon` IS THE SAUCE'S FACE IN THE BOOK, and only a sauce's
// last recipe carries one. The terminal product is a case, and a tree of
// identical cardboard boxes does not tell ketchup from BBQ at a glance, so
// the tree node and the page heading draw the bottle instead. `icon` stays
// the case: it is what the belt, the dock and the contracts show.
//
// `bbq_mix` and `bbq` deliberately name NO icon. There is no open pail of
// BBQ anywhere in the asset set, and `assets/voxels/palette.ron` records
// why that is on purpose: nothing carries BBQ in an open container, so the
// only voxels painted `bbq_brown` are a bottle's contents and a case's
// print. Both ids are fluids in pipes, the Cookbook draws a stage's
// MACHINE rather than its intermediate, and an icon here would be a
// picture that exists nowhere in the game.
//
// The RON extension below lets an optional field be written as its value
// (`display_name: "BBQ"`) instead of `Some("BBQ")`. It changes nothing about
// what this file means; it keeps a content file reading like content.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
(
    schema_version: 3,
    recipes: [
        // Crush: whole tomatoes in, paste out. Tomatoes are a raw
        // ingredient — nothing here produces them, so they are bought
        // (`data/ingredients.ron`) and loaded into a hopper.
        Recipe(
            id: "tomato_paste",
            blurb: "Whole tomatoes in, paste out. The crusher is the only machine that eats a raw solid straight off a hopper with nothing upstream of it, and it takes several tomatoes to the pail — which is why a ketchup line burns more tomato than anything else on the shelf.",
            icon: "item_paste_pail",
            inputs: [("tomato", 4)],
            outputs: [("tomato_paste", 1)],
            machine: "crusher",
            time_ticks: 80,
        ),
        // Mix: paste, sugar and vinegar blended into an uncooked ketchup
        // mix — and the mix comes out as a **liquid**, into the pipe run off
        // the mixer's outlet face.
        //
        // Sugar is the chain's second raw ingredient (GAMEPLAY.md §2's tier
        // table has ketchup adding "vinegar, sugar, spices"), and the reason
        // a world now has more than one hopper in it: a hopper holds one
        // item id, so two raw solids means two hoppers, and *which* item a
        // hopper holds became something the player says when they place it.
        //
        // Vinegar is the one fluid the player supplies rather than
        // producing: it arrives from a `fluid_tank` the player loads, on its
        // own pipe run touching this machine somewhere other than its outlet
        // face.
        Recipe(
            id: "ketchup_mix",
            blurb: "Paste and sugar arrive on two belt lanes, vinegar arrives down a pipe, and what leaves is a liquid. This is where the chain stops being a conveyor: the mixer's outlet face wants a pipe run, not a belt.",
            icon: "item_mix_pail",
            inputs: [("tomato_paste", 1), ("sugar", 1)],
            fluid_inputs: [("vinegar", 2)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("ketchup_mix", 1)],
            machine: "mixer",
            time_ticks: 90,
        ),
        // Cook: the long batch reduction that turns the mix into finished
        // ketchup. Liquid in, liquid out — **no belt touches this machine at
        // all**, which is what makes it the clearest demonstration that a
        // stage's connections are content rather than geometry. It is the
        // slowest step in the chain and the reason the cooking vat is a 2x2
        // machine.
        Recipe(
            id: "ketchup",
            tagline: "The first sauce. Everything else in the book is built on it.",
            blurb: "The long cook, and the slowest step in a ketchup line — which makes the vat the bottleneck the whole line is shaped around. Liquid in, liquid out: no belt touches this machine at all, which is the clearest proof in the game that what connects a stage is content rather than geometry.",
            icon: "item_ketchup_pail",
            inputs: [],
            fluid_inputs: [("ketchup_mix", 1)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("ketchup", 1)],
            machine: "cooking_vat",
            time_ticks: 240,
        ),
        // Bottling: the stage where the product stops being a fluid. Liquid
        // ketchup is drawn from the pipe run and leaves as a solid bottle on
        // a belt — so this is the only recipe in the file with a fluid input
        // and a solid output, and it is where the piped middle of the chain
        // rejoins the conveyor.
        //
        // **ONE BATCH FILLS ONE CASE: 8 BOTTLES** (2026-10-02; it was one
        // bottle, so a "case" held a single bottle). A unit of fluid
        // everywhere upstream is a case-sized batch, and the multiplier
        // lives HERE rather than at the vat on purpose. Measured: with the
        // vat making 8 small units instead, this bottler sips them one at a
        // time and the BBQ mixer, which needs 8 at once off the same run,
        // never starts at all. Upstream of this line nothing moved, so the
        // bill per case, every contract, both gates and the 3:2 splits are
        // untouched.
        //
        // **200 TICKS IS THE BELT, NOT THE NOZZLES.** A fill deposits all 8
        // into an output buffer that holds exactly 8 (`world.rs`'s
        // `MACHINE_OUTPUT_CAPACITY`), and a belt lane takes one item per 25
        // ticks, so the buffer needs ~175 ticks to empty. A shorter fill
        // finishes into a buffer still holding bottles and the load lights
        // read "backed up" on a machine that is not (`tests/machine_load.rs`
        // caught that at 60). 200 clears it with margin and stays under the
        // vat's 240, so the vat is still the line's bottleneck.
        Recipe(
            id: "bottled_ketchup",
            blurb: "Where the product stops being a fluid. One batch of ketchup is drawn out of the pipe run and leaves as a whole case's worth of bottles on a belt — the one step in the game with a liquid input and a solid output, and where the piped middle of the chain rejoins the conveyor.",
            icon: "item_bottle",
            inputs: [],
            fluid_inputs: [("ketchup", 1)],
            outputs: [("bottled_ketchup", 8)],
            machine: "bottler",
            time_ticks: 200,
        ),
        // Added AFTER the 3-stage chain above was already working end to
        // end, purely by editing this file — no sim_core/src or
        // godot_project/*.gd changes accompanied that entry. This is
        // milestone 4's required proof that extending the chain is a
        // data-file-only change — no recipe id is baked into Rust anywhere,
        // then or now.
        Recipe(
            id: "boxed_ketchup",
            tagline: "What the dock ships, and what the contracts pay for.",
            blurb: "The packer is the end of the line; past it there is only belt and the dock. Nothing downstream cares which sauce is in the case — only the contract board does.",
            icon: "item_box",
            cookbook_icon: "item_bottle",
            inputs: [("bottled_ketchup", 8)],
            outputs: [("boxed_ketchup", 1)],
            machine: "packer",
            time_ticks: 40,
        ),

        // --- BBQ sauce: the second sauce, and the first BRANCH ---
        //
        // Four more stages, no new machine kind: each one is a second recipe
        // on a kind the ketchup line already uses. What makes them a branch
        // rather than a continuation is the first one's fluid input — it
        // draws `ketchup`, which the cooking vat above produces, so this is
        // a second path *off* the ketchup line rather than five more stages
        // on the end of it. GAMEPLAY.md §3 calls that a derived sauce.
        //
        // The whole point is the shared tank. The ketchup a player sells and
        // the ketchup they cook into BBQ come out of the same vat, so every
        // box of BBQ is a box of ketchup not shipped — which is what gives
        // GAMEPLAY.md §6's mixed contracts their teeth. Serving `grill_pub`
        // (`data/contracts.ron`) is not "run two lines", it is "build enough
        // ketchup for both".

        // BBQ mix: finished ketchup thickened and spiced. Liquid ketchup
        // arrives by pipe from the ketchup vat's run; spice mix and molasses
        // arrive on two belt lanes, exactly as paste and sugar do at the
        // ketchup mixer (and for the same reason the mixer is 1x2 — one
        // inlet tile per solid, `data/machines.ron`).
        //
        // 90 ticks, the same as `ketchup_mix`: it is the same machine doing
        // the same work, and a second blend time would be a number with no
        // story behind it.
        //
        // FIRST OF THE FOUR IDS THAT NAME THEMSELVES. The HUD titles an item
        // by title-casing its id, which is right for every other id in this
        // file ("ketchup_mix" -> "Ketchup Mix") and wrong for an acronym:
        // "bbq" came out as "Bbq" on the contract board and in the
        // production panel. `display_name` is the fix, and it lives here
        // rather than in a table in GDScript because a name is content. Omit
        // it and title-casing is what happens — the common case by far.
        Recipe(
            id: "bbq_mix",
            display_name: "BBQ Mix",
            blurb: "Finished ketchup, thickened with molasses and spice. Two belt lanes and a pipe run feed one mixer here, which is the first time the factory has to get a fluid and two solids into the same machine at once.",
            inputs: [("spice_mix", 1), ("molasses", 1)],
            fluid_inputs: [("ketchup", 1)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("bbq_mix", 1)],
            machine: "mixer",
            time_ticks: 90,
        ),
        // The BBQ cook: the longest process in the game at 600 ticks, and
        // the first recipe to draw **two** fluids — the mix off the BBQ
        // mixer's pipe run, and liquid smoke out of a tank the player fills.
        // Two draws is what `sim_core`'s fluid scope was widened for; each
        // is matched to a touching pipe run by fluid id, and the one deposit
        // still leaves by the outlet face.
        //
        // **600 IS LOAD-BEARING, AND IT IS A RATIO, NOT A FLAVOUR NOTE.**
        // At a 600-tick cook this branch draws one ketchup per 600 ticks
        // from a ketchup line that makes one per 240 (the vat above is that
        // line's bottleneck), so the remainder bottles out as ketchup at one
        // per 400. One ketchup line plus one BBQ branch therefore settles at
        // **3 ketchup : 2 BBQ** — and `grill_pub` is a 3 + 2 order for
        // exactly that reason, so the obvious factory serves the first mixed
        // contract without tuning, and every larger mixed order on the board
        // is the same shape scaled up.
        //
        // So this number and those contract mixes move together or not at
        // all: change the cook and `data/contracts.ron`'s mixed orders stop
        // matching what a factory naturally makes, and the claim above (and
        // in `docs/BBQ_SAUCE_PLAN.md` §2.1) becomes false. Same rule the
        // crush/blend/cook ordering already states above.
        Recipe(
            id: "bbq",
            display_name: "BBQ",
            tagline: "The second sauce, and the first thing you make out of something you already make.",
            blurb: "The longest process in the game, and deliberately so. A BBQ vat draws its ketchup from the same run that feeds the ketchup bottler, so every case of BBQ is a case of ketchup not shipped. A factory feeding both settles at about three ketchup to two BBQ — which is the shape of every mixed order on the contract board.",
            inputs: [],
            fluid_inputs: [("bbq_mix", 1), ("liquid_smoke", 1)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("bbq", 1)],
            machine: "cooking_vat",
            time_ticks: 600,
        ),
        // Bottling, the BBQ side: liquid in, a case's worth of bottles out on
        // the belt, 200 ticks — the mirror of `bottled_ketchup` on a second
        // bottler, for the same reasons.
        Recipe(
            id: "bottled_bbq",
            display_name: "Bottled BBQ",
            blurb: "The mirror of the ketchup bottler on a second machine: same work, darker glass. A belt carrying BBQ has to be tellable from a belt carrying ketchup at a glance, so the bottle is its own model rather than a tinted one.",
            icon: "item_bottle_bbq",
            inputs: [],
            fluid_inputs: [("bbq", 1)],
            outputs: [("bottled_bbq", 8)],
            machine: "bottler",
            time_ticks: 200,
        ),
        // Packing, the BBQ side: 40 ticks, and `boxed_bbq` is the second
        // thing this factory can sell. A box rides ordinary belt into
        // whichever dock that belt feeds — a dock does not care which sauce
        // a box holds, only `data/contracts.ron` does.
        Recipe(
            id: "boxed_bbq",
            display_name: "Boxed BBQ",
            blurb: "The second thing this factory can sell. A case rides ordinary belt into whichever dock that belt feeds — a dock does not care which sauce a case holds, only the contract board does.",
            icon: "item_box_bbq",
            cookbook_icon: "item_bottle_bbq",
            inputs: [("bottled_bbq", 8)],
            outputs: [("boxed_bbq", 1)],
            machine: "packer",
            time_ticks: 40,
        ),

        // --- Mustard: the third sauce, and the first whole SECOND LINE ---
        //
        // Five more stages, no new machine kind — again — but where the BBQ
        // entries above hang off the middle of the ketchup line, these ones
        // start at a hopper of their own and run all the way to the dock.
        // Doing "a branch off a sauce" twice would teach nothing new; a
        // second line teaches the thing a branch cannot, which is that a
        // factory is a floor with a finite amount of room, belt and crew on
        // it.
        //
        // **What is actually new is one shared id: `vinegar`.** The mustard
        // crusher drinks the same bought fluid the ketchup mixer does, out of
        // one ledger and one storage allowance, so this is the first time two
        // products compete for a raw rather than for floor space. A player
        // who pipes one tank to both machines starves whichever one is
        // downstream, and a player who buys for one line has not bought for
        // the other. `data/ingredients.ron`'s header note (point 6) is the
        // same claim seen from the shop's side.
        //
        // The two lines share the raw and NOT the machine kind that drinks
        // it, which is the header's one-raw-fluid-per-kind rule: the shop-side
        // competition is the lesson, and it survives intact with the drum
        // plumbed to the crusher instead of the mixer.
        //
        // It is also why this is *nearly* a disconnected component of the
        // recipe graph and not quite one: nothing here consumes anything the
        // ketchup entries produce, but `vinegar` ties the two lines
        // together, so `validate_chain` still sees one graph. Mayonnaise
        // (GAMEPLAY.md §2) is the one that will finally be an island.

        // Mill, the mustard side: seed and vinegar in, wet mash out, 80
        // ticks — the same machine doing the same work as `tomato_paste`, so
        // the same number. This is the second recipe on the crusher and the
        // reason the kind needed more than one at all.
        //
        // **THE VINEGAR IS DRUNK HERE, AND WHICH MACHINE DRINKS IT IS NOT A
        // FLAVOUR DECISION.** Mustard is milled wet in the real world, which
        // is why this reads right — but the reason it is not on the mixer
        // below is a hard constraint of the simulation, stated in the header
        // note "AT MOST ONE RAW FLUID PER MACHINE KIND". `ketchup_mix`
        // already drinks `vinegar` on the mixer; a second mixer recipe
        // drinking any raw fluid leaves a pipe run the narrowing pass cannot
        // name, and the SHIPPED KETCHUP LINE stalls on `AmbiguousPipe` in a
        // new game. The crusher drinks nothing else, so it is where a raw
        // fluid can still enter this content without colliding.
        Recipe(
            id: "mustard_mash",
            blurb: "Mustard is milled wet: seed and vinegar go in together and a coarse yellow mash comes out down a pipe. The crusher does not care that it is not a tomato — it is the same mechanical work, with a drum plumbed to it.",
            inputs: [("mustard_seed", 4)],
            fluid_inputs: [("vinegar", 2)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("mustard_mash", 1)],
            machine: "crusher",
            time_ticks: 80,
        ),
        // Mix, the mustard side: turmeric on a belt, mash down a pipe,
        // liquid mix out of the outlet face. 90 ticks, like both other
        // blends — same machine, same work, and a third blend time would be
        // a number with no story.
        //
        // What this recipe drinks is `mustard_mash`, a **derived** fluid,
        // for the reason spelled out above and in the header: every fluid
        // entering a kind that already has a raw one has to be something the
        // factory made. `bbq_mix` drinking finished `ketchup` is the same
        // move; this is the second instance, and the rule is now a test
        // (`recipe.rs`'s `at_most_one_raw_fluid_enters_any_machine_kind`).
        Recipe(
            id: "mustard_mix",
            blurb: "Turmeric arrives on a belt and the milled mash arrives down a pipe, and what leaves the outlet face is mustard in everything but the cooking. The vinegar this sauce fights ketchup over is already in the mash.",
            inputs: [("turmeric", 1)],
            fluid_inputs: [("mustard_mash", 1)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("mustard_mix", 1)],
            machine: "mixer",
            time_ticks: 90,
        ),
        // The mustard cook. Liquid in, liquid out, no belt on the machine —
        // the ketchup cook's shape on a second vat.
        //
        // **360 IS LOAD-BEARING, AND LIKE 600 IT IS A RATIO RATHER THAN A
        // FLAVOUR NOTE.** A mustard line's bottleneck is this vat, exactly as
        // a ketchup line's is its own, so a factory that has finished one of
        // each produces one case per 240 ticks on the ketchup side and one
        // per 360 on this one. That settles at **3 cases of ketchup to 2 of
        // mustard** — which is the mix `deli_chain` asks for in
        // `data/contracts.ron`, for precisely the reason `grill_pub` is a
        // 3 + 2 order on the BBQ side. The obvious two-line factory serves
        // the mustard tier's mixed order without tuning anything.
        //
        // So this number and that contract's mix move together or not at
        // all: change the cook and `deli_chain` stops describing what a
        // factory naturally makes, and both files' comments become false.
        // Same rule as the crush/blend/cook ordering and the BBQ 600 above.
        //
        // Why longer than ketchup's cook at all: it is the one process time
        // on this line that is not simply copied from the ketchup stage
        // doing the same job, and it has to not be, or two finished lines
        // would settle at 1:1 and a mixed order would have nothing to say.
        // Longer than ketchup and far shorter than BBQ also puts mustard
        // where it belongs on the board — a second line is more sauce than a
        // branch is, but it is not the premium product.
        Recipe(
            id: "mustard",
            tagline: "The third sauce, and the first one that is a factory of its own rather than a branch off one.",
            blurb: "A longer cook than ketchup's and a far shorter one than BBQ's, which is what makes a two-line factory settle at roughly three cases of ketchup to two of mustard — the shape of the mixed order on the mustard tier of the board. Liquid in, liquid out, and not a belt in sight.",
            inputs: [],
            fluid_inputs: [("mustard_mix", 1)],
            outputs: [],
            fluid_outputs: [("mustard", 1)],
            machine: "cooking_vat",
            time_ticks: 360,
        ),
        // Bottling, the mustard side: 8 bottles in 200 ticks, the third
        // bottler recipe and the same trade as the other two — the pipe run
        // ends here and the product goes back on a belt as a solid.
        Recipe(
            id: "bottled_mustard",
            blurb: "The third bottler recipe and the same trade as the other two: the pipe run ends, and the product goes back on a belt as something you can pick up. A belt of mustard has to be tellable from a belt of ketchup at a glance, so it gets its own bottle rather than a tinted one.",
            icon: "item_bottle_mustard",
            inputs: [],
            fluid_inputs: [("mustard", 1)],
            outputs: [("bottled_mustard", 8)],
            machine: "bottler",
            time_ticks: 200,
        ),
        // Packing, the mustard side: 40 ticks, and `boxed_mustard` is the
        // third thing this factory can sell. Every line's cases ride
        // ordinary belt into the same dock, and the delivery pool still does
        // not care which sauce is in one.
        Recipe(
            id: "boxed_mustard",
            blurb: "The third thing this factory can sell, and the first that arrives at the dock without a single tomato behind it. Three lines, one delivery pool: only the contract board knows the difference.",
            icon: "item_box_mustard",
            cookbook_icon: "item_bottle_mustard",
            inputs: [("bottled_mustard", 8)],
            outputs: [("boxed_mustard", 1)],
            machine: "packer",
            time_ticks: 40,
        ),
    ],
)
